the track: 'no sibling .test.ts' turned out not to mean 'untested'.
DataDirectoryInitializer was already at 90% via a non-sibling file, and
WantedService reports 100% only because it has zero branches. Two of seven
targets needed no coverage work at all.

DEVIATION
Phases 2-5 specified Red-then-Green per size bucket. Both halves were wrong:
buckets were sized by LOC while the real gaps sat elsewhere, and a Red phase is
not meaningful for coverage tests over already-working code -- they pass on
first run by construction. Writing them to fail first would mean asserting
things the code does not do. Genuine Red moments are listed in the plan.

FINDINGS (all logged in tech-debt.md, none silently absorbed)
1. LibraryScanner scan-abort -- CONFIRMED BY TEST AND LIVE. One unreadable
   subdirectory rejects the entire scan; because getAllFiles collects every
   path before parsing any, an abort links NOTHING, not even files already
   enumerated from readable directories. Pinned by characterisation tests.
2. MetadataProvider computes tmdbCollectionId in searchMovies; all three
   searchMedia mappings drop it. searchMovies is private and called only from
   searchMedia, so the computation is dead.
3. SeriesSearchResult omits 'popularity' although searchSeries always sets it
   -- caught by tsc, not by tests -- which is why production reads it back
   through (result as any).
4. Local metadata-sidecar generation is unimplemented, not merely unwired:
   Organizer.colocateMovieMetadata is also never called in production.

DELETION
MetadataGenerator had no production callers. The owner wanted it gone but was
concerned about losing artwork; verified false three ways before deleting --
posters are remote TMDB URLs rendered straight from the CDN, downloadPoster
never ran, and its .nfo path was duplicated by Organizer. Two real defects died
with it (unguarded escapeXml; Buffer.from(body,'binary') on an already
UTF-8-decoded string, corrupting every image) and are recorded so they are not
reintroduced.

